---
import Prose from '@/layouts/Prose.astro';
import { SITE } from '@/site';
import { tools } from '@/tools/registry';

// TODO(owner): set SITE.owner.name in src/site.ts to put a name on this page; it reads fine without one
const owner = SITE.owner.name;
const toolCount = tools.filter((t) => !t.variantOf).length;
---

<Prose
  title="About Rounder: who makes it, and why"
  description="Rounder is a free, open-source workshop of browser tools, made by one independent developer fed up with fake download buttons. Why it exists and how it's built."
  heading="About <em>rounder</em>"
>
  <p>
    Rounder is a workshop of {toolCount} free tools for video, images, PDFs, text and everyday jobs. Every one of them runs in
    your browser, so your files never leave your device. No sign-ups, no watermarks, no daily limits.
  </p>
  <p>
    {owner ? <>It's made by <strong>{owner}</strong>, one independent developer.</> : <>It's made by one independent developer.</>}
    No company, no investors, no growth team.
  </p>

  <h2>Why it exists</h2>
  <p>
    Rounder started in the middle of editing a video montage. I needed a few small jobs done: trim a clip, convert a format,
    shrink a file so it could be sent. Every site I tried was the same: upload your file to a stranger's server, wait in a
    queue, dodge three fake "Download" buttons, then hit a sign-up wall, a watermark or a "premium" limit right before the
    file I actually needed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Browsers can do almost all of this work themselves now, on your own device, for free. So I started building the tools
    I wished existed, then kept going. Rounder is the result: the tool, done properly, and nothing in the way.
  </p>

  <h2>How it's built</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Everything runs locally.</strong> Tools use WebAssembly, Web Workers, WebCodecs and WebGPU to do the heavy work on your device. There's no server that processes files. The only server code stores the optional <a href="/privacy#ratings">tool ratings</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>On the shoulders of open source.</strong> The engines are well-known libraries such as PDF.js, pdf-lib, FFmpeg and ONNX Runtime, and AI models released openly by researchers. They're all on the <a href="/credits">credits page</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Tested in real browsers.</strong> Automated tests open every page in Chrome, Firefox and Safari's engine, at desktop and phone sizes, and run real files through the tools.</li>
    <li><strong>Simple hosting.</strong> The site is a set of static files on Cloudflare, plus that small ratings API. <a href="/security">Here's every network request it makes.</a></li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Built with AI help</h2>
  <p>
    I build Rounder with AI coding assistants, mainly Anthropic's Claude, which write a large share of the code and help draft
    the pages. I decide what each tool should do, review the work, test the tools and fix what's wrong, and I'm responsible for
    everything on the site. If something is broken or wrong, that's on me, and I'd like to <a href="/contact">hear about it</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    That's separate from the AI <em>tools</em> on the site, like the background remover or the transcriber. Those run open
    models on your own device, and nothing you give them is used to train anything.
  </p>

  <h2>Open source</h2>
  <p>
    The whole site is open source under the <a href="https://www.gnu.org/licenses/agpl-3.0.html" rel="noopener">AGPL-3.0</a>.
    You can read the code at <a href={SITE.github}>{SITE.github.replace('https://', '')}</a>, check that it does what this
    site says, suggest a tool, fix a bug or run your own copy.
  </p>

  <h2>How it stays free</h2>
  <p>
    A few polite ads from Google pay for the domain and the time. The tools that handle passwords, keys and private documents
    have no ads at all. <a href="/why-free">More on why it's free.</a>
  </p>

  <h2>Say hello</h2>
  <p>
    Questions, bug reports and tool ideas are all welcome: email <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a> or see the
    <a href="/contact">contact page</a>. Writing about Rounder? There's a <a href="/press">press kit</a>.
  </p>
</Prose>
